Paper 6

Title: A Systematic Review of Green and Sustainable AI: Taxonomy, Metrics, Challenges, and Open Research Directions

Link: https://doi.org/10.3390/su18084115

Existing Method:
The paper reviews different Green AI techniques, sustainability metrics, and AI optimization methods used to reduce energy consumption.

Advantages:

Comprehensive review of Green AI.
Covers different sustainability metrics.
Highlights current challenges.
Identifies future research directions.

Limitations:

No practical implementation.
Limited focus on cloud infrastructure.
No real-time carbon monitoring.

Research Gap:
The paper does not provide a cloud-based framework for continuous carbon monitoring and sustainability management.

Possible Improvement:
Develop an AI-powered cloud framework that continuously tracks carbon emissions and provides optimization recommendations.

---

Paper 7

Title: Strategies and Design for Increasing AI Sustainability

Link: https://www.nature.com/articles/s44359-026-00195-w

Existing Method:
The paper discusses strategies for designing energy-efficient and sustainable AI systems.

Advantages:

Encourages sustainable AI practices.
Focuses on reducing energy consumption.
Promotes responsible AI development.

Limitations:

Mostly theoretical.
No cloud implementation.
No AI prediction model.

Research Gap:
The study lacks an automated cloud solution for monitoring and predicting carbon emissions.

Possible Improvement:
Integrate Green AI with cloud services to monitor, analyze, and reduce carbon emissions automatically.

---


Paper 8

Title: Beyond the Carbon Emissions of Artificial Intelligence (AI): A Whole-Systems Energy and Environmental Sustainability Analysis of Datacenters in Denmark, Germany and Norway

Link: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214629626003105

Existing Method:
The authors evaluate the environmental impact of AI-enabled data centers using a whole-systems analysis.

Advantages:

Detailed environmental analysis.
Considers renewable energy usage.
Supports sustainability planning.

Limitations:

Focuses only on data centers.
No cloud resource monitoring.
No optimization recommendations.

Research Gap:
The study does not provide resource-level carbon tracking or intelligent cloud optimization.

Possible Improvement:
Develop a Digital Carbon Passport system for individual cloud resources with AI-based recommendations.

---

Paper 9

Title: Digital Carbon Footprint Assessment of Universitas Indonesia Data Center Based on the GHG Protocol

Link: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43621-026-03522-0

Existing Method:
The paper estimates data center carbon emissions using the Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Protocol.

Advantages:

Uses standard carbon calculation methods.
Provides accurate emission estimates.
Supports sustainability assessment.

Limitations:

Limited to one case study.
No AI integration.
No real-time monitoring.

Research Gap:
The paper lacks predictive analytics and automated sustainability monitoring.

Possible Improvement:
Combine carbon assessment with AI models and cloud monitoring services for continuous carbon tracking.

---

Paper 10

Title: A Comprehensive Review of Digital Product Passports in Sustainable Manufacturing: Current State, Challenges, and Future Directions

Link: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43615-026-00927-x

Existing Method:
The paper reviews Digital Product Passport technologies used in manufacturing industries.

Advantages:

Explains Digital Product Passport concepts.
Supports sustainability and traceability.
Encourages circular economy practices.

Limitations:

Limited to manufacturing.
Not applicable to cloud infrastructure.
No AI-based analytics.

Research Gap:
The concept of Digital Product Passports has not been extended to cloud infrastructure for carbon monitoring.

Possible Improvement:
Develop AI-powered Digital Carbon Passports for cloud resources to improve sustainability management.
